"TensorZKP: Repurposing GPU Tensor Cores for High-Performance Zero-Knowledge Proofs | USENIX (https://www.usenix.org/conference/usenixsecurity26/presentation/lu-tao)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.usenix.org/conference/usenixsecurity26/presentation/lu-tao\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 35\nL0: cite0†Back to USENIX L1: \nL2:   * cite1†Sign In L3:   * cite2†Conferences L4: # TensorZKP: Repurposing GPU Tensor Cores for High-Performance Zero-Knowledge Proofs\nL5: \nL6: Tao Lu, Jipeng Zhang, and Yanpei Guo, National University of Singapore; Xuanming Liu, Zhejiang University; Wenjie Qu, National University of Singapore; Zonghui Wang and Wenzhi Chen, Zhejiang University; Jiaheng Zhang, National University of Singapore\nL7: GPU Tensor Cores, specialized hardware units designed to accelerate matrix multiplication, have served as the primary engine behind the AI revolution. Given the exponential performance gains they have delivered, aligning cryptographic implementations with this hardware evolution is critical. This is particularly acute for zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs), a cryptographic primitive that currently grapples with high proof generation costs.\nL8: Existing GPU implementations for ZKPs rely exclusively on general-purpose SIMT cores, leaving the massive computational power of Tensor Cores untapped.\nL9: In this paper, we introduce TensorZKP, the first GPU framework to harness Tensor Cores for ZKP acceleration. Since Tensor Cores are designed for low-precision matrix multiplication, mapping ZKP's arithmetic to this hardware is non-trivial. To bridge this gap, we develop Tensor-Core-compatible finite field arithmetic and reformulate ZKP modules, specifically sum-check protocols and Spielman code, into matrix multiplication tasks.\nL10: Furthermore, we design an asynchronous warp-specialized framework that pipelines memory access, Tensor Core matrix operations, and SIMT-based modular reductions. We instantiate these optimizations with HyperPlonk as the Polynomial Interactive Oracle Proof (PIOP) and Brakedown as the Polynomial Commitment Scheme (PCS) to enable end-to-end proof generation.\nL11: The evaluation results show that TensorZKP exhibits remarkable efficiency. At a 2^25 scale, the underlying building blocks complete in 0.85 ms for inner product, 0.91 ms for scalar-vector multiplication, 4.04 ms for degree-2 sum-check, and 11.58 ms for the encoder.\nL12: For a circuit with 2^25 multiplication gates, TensorZKP achieves a proof generation time of only 215.28 milliseconds, representing a 955× speedup over the CPU baseline and a 36.2× improvement over state-of-the-art SIMT-based GPU implementations.\nL13: USENIX Security '26 Open Access Sponsored by\nL14: King Abdullah University of Science and Technology (KAUST)\nL15: ## Open Access Media\nL16: \nL17: USENIX is committed to Open Access to the research presented at our events. Papers and proceedings are freely available to everyone once the event begins. Any video, audio, and/or slides that are posted after the event are also free and open to everyone. cite3†Support USENIX and our commitment to Open Access.\nL18: \nL19: BibTeX\nL20: @inproceedings {320867,\nL21: author = {Tao Lu and Jipeng Zhang and Yanpei Guo and Xuanming Liu and Wenjie Qu and Zonghui Wang and Wenzhi Chen and Jiaheng Zhang},\nL22: title = {{TensorZKP}: Repurposing {GPU} Tensor Cores for {High-Performance} {Zero-Knowledge} Proofs},\nL23: booktitle = {35th USENIX Security Symposium (USENIX Security 26)},\nL24: year = {2026},\nL25: isbn = {978-1-939133-58-8},\nL26: address = {Baltimore, MD},\nL27: pages = {5849--5867},\nL28: url = {https://www.usenix.org/conference/usenixsecurity26/presentation/lu-tao},\nL29: publisher = {USENIX Association},\nL30: month = aug\nL31: }\nL32: cite4†Download L33: \nL34: cite5†Image: application/pdf cite6†Lu PDF --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2604.17808] Enabling AI ASICs for Zero Knowledge Proof (https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.17808)\nciteturn1view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.17808\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 165\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Hardware Architecture\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 20 Apr 2026]\nL9: # Title:Enabling AI ASICs for Zero Knowledge Proof\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Jianming Tong , cite6†Jingtian Dang , cite7†Simon Langowski , cite8†Tianhao Huang , cite9†Asra Ali , cite10†Jeremy Kun , cite11†Jevin Jiang , cite12†Srinivas Devadas , cite13†Tushar Krishna L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Enabling AI ASICs for Zero Knowledge Proof, by Jianming Tong and 7 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite14†View PDF cite15†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Zero-knowledge proof (ZKP) provers remain costly because multi-scalar multiplication (MSM) and number-theoretic transforms (NTTs) dominate runtime as they need significant computation. AI ASICs such as TPUs provide massive matrix throughput and SotA energy efficiency. We present MORPH, the first framework that reformulates ZKP kernels to match AI-ASIC execution.\nL17: We introduce Big-T complexity, a hardware-aware complexity model that exposes heterogeneous bottlenecks and layout-transformation costs ignored by Big-O.\nL18: Guided by this analysis, (1) at arithmetic level, MORPH develops an MXU-centric extended-RNS lazy reduction that converts high-precision modular arithmetic into dense low-precision GEMMs, eliminating all carry chains, and (2) at dataflow level, MORPH constructs a unified-sharding layout-stationary TPU Pippenger MSM and optimized 3/5-step NTT that avoid on-TPU shuffles to minimize costly memory reorganization.\nL19: Implemented in JAX, MORPH enables TPUv6e8 to achieve up-to 10x higher throughput on NTT and comparable throughput on MSM than GZKP. Our code: cite16†this https URL†github.com .\nL20: Comments:  | Design Automation Conference 2026\nL21: Subjects:  | Hardware Architecture (cs.AR); Computation and Language (cs.CL); Cryptography and Security (cs.CR); Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS); Programming Languages (cs.PL)\nL22: Cite as:  | cite17†arXiv:2604.17808 [cs.AR]\nL23:    | (or cite18†arXiv:2604.17808v1 [cs.AR] for this version)\nL24:    | cite19†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2604.17808†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: ## Submission history\nL26: \nL27: From: Jianming Tong [cite20†view email ]\nL28: [v1] Mon, 20 Apr 2026 05:00:47 UTC (1,566 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Enabling AI ASICs for Zero Knowledge Proof, by Jianming Tong and 7 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite14†View PDF L37:   * cite15†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite21†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite22†view license†creativecommons.org L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.AR\nL44: \nL45: cite23†< prev |   cite24†next > L46: \nL47: cite25†new | cite26†recent | cite27†2026-04 L48: \nL49: Change to browse by:\nL50: \nL51: cite28†cs L52: cite29†cs.CL L53: cite30†cs.CR L54: cite31†cs.DS L55: cite32†cs.PL L56: \nL57: ### References & Citations\nL58: \nL59:   * cite33†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L60:   * cite34†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L61:   * cite35†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L62: \nL63: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL64: \nL65: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL66: \nL67: [Button: ×]\nL68: \nL69: loading...\nL70: \nL71: Data provided by:\nL72: \nL73: ### Bookmark\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL76: # Bibliographic and Citation T