* cite7†Listing by year L13:     * cite8†All papers L14:     * cite9†Compact view L15:     * cite10†Subscribe†www.iacr.org L16:     * * * *\nL17: \nL18:     * cite11†How to cite L19:     * * * *\nL20:     * cite12†Harvesting metadata L21:   * cite2†Submissions L22:     * cite13†Submit a paper L23:     * cite14†Revise or withdraw a paper L24:     * cite15†Acceptance and publishing conditions L25: \nL26: cite16†Image: Search Button L27: \nL28: [Input] [Button: Search ]\nL29: #### Paper 2024/1075\nL30: \nL31: ### cite17†TaSSLE: Lasso for the commitment-phobic L32: \nL33: Tesseract Dore\nL34: ##### Abstract\nL35: We present TaSSLE, a new lookup argument for decomposable tables with minimal commitment costs. The construction generalizes techniques introduced in Lasso (Eurocrypt '24) which take advantage of the internal structure present in such tables to avoid the need for any party to need to commit to, or even construct, the entire table. This allows the use of lookups against very large tables, with applications including new design strategies for \"zero-knowledge virtual machines\".\nL36: We show that these techniques may be combined in a generic way with any existing lookup argument to achieve similar results. We then give a construction of TaSSLE by applying this observation to a recent lookup argument, introduced in [Papini-Haböck '23], which combines logarithmic derivatives with the GKR protocol to achieve a lookup argument with minimal commitment costs.\nL37: ##### Metadata\nL38: \nL39: Available format(s)\nL40:     cite17†PDF L41: Category\nL42:     cite18†Cryptographic protocols L43: Publication info\nL44:     Preprint.\nL45: Keywords\nL46:     cite19†lookup argument cite20†proof system cite21†iop L47: Contact author(s)\nL48:     dorebell @ gmail com\nL49: History\nL50:     2025-03-08: revised\nL51:     2024-07-02: received\nL52:     cite22†See all versions L53: Short URL\nL54:     cite23†https://ia.cr/2024/1075†ia.cr L55: License\nL56: \nL57: cite24†CC BY†creativecommons.org L58: BibTeX cite25†Image Copy to clipboard\nL59: \nL60:     @misc{cryptoeprint:2024/1075,\nL61:           author = {Tesseract Dore},\nL62:           title = {{TaSSLE}: Lasso for the commitment-phobic},\nL63:           howpublished = {Cryptology {ePrint} Archive, Paper 2024/1075},\nL64:           year = {2024},\nL65:           url = {https://eprint.iacr.org/2024/1075}\nL66:     }\nL67: \nL68: \nL69: Note: In order to protect the privacy of readers, eprint.iacr.org does not use cookies or embedded third party content.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSumcheck API Documentation | ICICLE Docs (https://dev.ingonyama.com/api/cpp/sumcheck)\nciteturn1view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://dev.ingonyama.com/api/cpp/sumcheck\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 246\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2: 🚀 We just released ICICLE v4 — featuring a more intuitive API with lattices. cite1†Check out the migration guide .\nL3: \nL4:   * cite2†4.0.0 L5:   * cite3†3.9.0 L6:   * cite4†3.8.0 L7:   * cite5†3.7.0 L8:   * cite6†3.6.0 L9:   * cite7†3.5.0 L10:   * cite8†3.4.0 L11:   * cite9†3.3.0 L12:   * cite10†3.2.0 L13:   * cite11†3.1.0 L14:   * cite12†3.0.0 L15:   * cite13†2.8.0 L16:   * cite14†1.10.1 L17: \nL18: Search\nL19: \nL20:   * cite15†API Overview L21:   * cite16†C++ L22:     * cite16†C++ Overview L23:     * cite17†MSM L24:     * cite18†NTT / ECNTT L25:     * cite19†Vector operations L26:     * cite20†Program L27:     * cite21†Polynomials L28:     * cite22†Hash L29:     * cite23†Merkle-Tree L30:     * cite2†Sumcheck L31:     * cite24†FRI L32:     * cite25†Pairings L33:     * cite26†PQC ML-KEM L34:     * cite27†Serialization L35:     * cite28†Matrix Operations L36:   * cite29†Golang Bindings L37:     * cite29†Golang Overview L38:     * cite30†MSM L39:     * cite31†MSM pre-computation L40:     * cite32†NTT L41:     * cite33†EC-NTT L42:     * cite34†Vector operations L43:     * cite35†Multi GPU Support L44:     * cite36†Hash L45:     * cite37†Merkle-Tree L46:     * cite38†PQC ML-KEM L47:   * cite39†Rust Bindings L48:     * cite39†Rust Overview L49:     * cite40†MSM L50:     * cite41†NTT L51:     * cite42†ECNTT L52:     * cite43†Vector operations L53:     * cite44†Program L54:     * cite45†Polynomials L55:     * cite46†Hash L56:     * cite47†Merkle-Tree L57:     * cite48†Sumcheck L58:     * cite49†FRI L59:     * cite50†Lattice SNARKs L60:     * cite51†PQC ML-KEM L61:     * cite52†Serialization L62:     * cite53†Pairings L63:     * cite54†Matrix Operations L64: \nL65: Version: 4.0.0\nL66: \nL67: [Button: On this page]\nL68: # Sumcheck API Documentation\nL69: ## Overviewcite55†​ L70: \nL71: The Sumcheck protocol allows a Prover to prove to a Verifier that the sum of a multilinear polynomial, over the Boolean hypercube, equals a specific scalar value.\nL72: \nL73: For a polynomial $P$P with $n$n variables, the Prover aims to prove that:\nL74: \nL75: $$\\sum_{X_1 \\in \\{0, 1\\}}\\sum_{X_2 \\in \\{0, 1\\}}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot\\sum_{X_n \\in \\{0, 1\\}} P(X_1, X_2,..., X_n) = C,$$X 1​∈{0,1}∑​X 2​∈{0,1}∑​⋅⋅⋅X n​∈{0,1}∑​P(X 1​,X 2​,...,X n​)=C,\nL76: \nL77: where $C$C is some scalar.\nL78: The proof is constructed interactively, involving a series of $n$n rounds. Each round consists of a challenge (from the Verifier), a computation (from the Prover), and a verification step (by the Verifier). Using a Fiat-Shamir (FS) scheme, the proof becomes non-interactive, enabling the Prover to generate the entire proof and send it to the Verifier for validation.\nL79: ### Sumcheck with Combine Functioncite56†​ L80: \nL81: The Sumcheck protocol can be generalized to handle multiple multilinear polynomials. In this case, assuming there are $m$m polynomials of $n$n variables, and $f$f is an arbitrary function that takes these $m$m polynomials and returns a polynomial of equal or higher degree, the Prover tries to prove that:\nL82: $$\\sum_{X_1 \\in \\{0, 1\\}}\\sum_{X_2 \\in \\{0, 1\\}}\\cdot\\cdot\\cdot\\sum_{X_n \\in \\{0, 1\\}} f\\left(P_1(X_1, ..., X_n), P_2(X_1, ..., X_n), ..., P_m(X_1, ..., X_n)\\right) = C,$$X 1​∈{0,1}∑​X 2​∈{0,1}∑​⋅⋅⋅X n​∈{0,1}∑​f(P 1​(X 1​,...,X n​),P 2​(X 1​,...,X n​),...,P m​(X 1​,...,X n​))=C,\nL83: \nL84: where $C$C is some scalar.\nL85: ## ICICLE's Sumecheck Implementationcite57†​ L86: \nL87: ICICLE implements a non-interactive Sumcheck protocol that supports a combine function. It is available on both the CPU and CUDA backends of ICICLE. The polynomials are passed to the protocol in MLE (evaluation representation) form.\nL88: ### Implementation Limitationscite58†​ L89: \nL90: There are some limitations and assumptions in the Sumcheck implementation:\nL91:   * The maximum size of the polynomials (i.e., the number of evaluations $2^n$2 n) depends on the number of polynomials and the memory size of the device (e.g., CPU/GPU) being used. For example, with 4 polynomials, a GPU with 24GB of memory should be able to handle Sumcheck for polynomials of size up to $2^29$2 2 9.\nL92:   * The polynomial size must a power of 2.\nL93:   * The current implementation does not support generating the challenge ($\\alpha$α) in an extension field. This functionality is necessary for ensuring security when working with small fields.\nL94: ## C++ APIcite59†​ L95: \nL96: A Sumcheck object can be created using the following function:\nL97: \nL98:     `Sumcheck<scalar_t> create_sumcheck()\nL99:     `\nL100: \nL101: There are two key configuration structs related to the Sumcheck protocol.\nL102: ### SumcheckConfigcite60†​ L103: \nL104: The `SumcheckConfig` struct is used to specify parameters for the Sumcheck protocol. It contains the following fields:\nL105:   * `stream: icicleStreamHandle`: The CUDA stream for asynchronous execution. If `nullptr`, the default stream is used.\nL106:   * `use_extension_field: bool`: If true, an extension field is used for Fiat-Shamir results. Currently unsupported (should always be false).\nL107:   * `are_inputs_on_device: bool`: If true, the input polynomials are expected to reside on the device (e.g., GPU); otherwise, they are expected to reside on the host (e.g., CPU).\nL108:   * `is_async: bool`: If true runs the hash asynchronously.\nL109:   * `ext: ConfigExtension*`: Backend-specific extensions.\nL110: The default values are:\nL111: \nL112:     `  struct SumcheckConfig {\nL113:         icicleStreamHandle stream = nullptr;\nL114:         bool use_extension_field = false;\nL115:         uint64_t batch = 1;\nL116:         bool are_inputs_on_device =false;\nL117:         bool is_async = false;\nL118:         ConfigExtension* ext = nullptr;\nL119:       };\nL120:     `\nL121: ### SumcheckTranscriptConfigcite61†​ L122: \nL123: The `SumcheckTranscriptConfig<F>` class is used to specify parameters for the Fiat-Shamir scheme used by the Sumcheck protocol. It contains the following fields:\nL124:   * `hasher: Hash`: The hash function used to generate randomness for Fiat-Shamir.\nL125:   * `domain_label: char*`: The label for the domain separator in the transcript.\nL126:   * `poly_label: char*`: The label for round polynomials in the transcript.\nL127:   * `challenge_label: char*`: The label for round challenges in the transcript.\nL128:   * `seed: F`: The seed for initializing the RNG.\nL129:   * `little_endian: bool`: The encoding endianness.\nL130: There are three constructors for `SumcheckTranscriptConfig<F>`, each with its own default values:\nL131: \nL132:   * Default constructor:\nL133: \nL134:     `    SumcheckTranscriptConfig()\nL135:             : m_little_endian(true), m_seed_rng(F::from(0)), m_hasher(std::move(create_keccak_256_hash()))\nL136:     `\nL137:   * Constructor with byte vector for labels:\nL138: \nL139:     `    SumcheckTranscriptConfig(\nL140:           Hash hasher,\nL141:           std::vector<std::byte>&& domain_label,\nL142:           std::vector<std::byte>&& poly_label,\nL143:           std::vector<std::byte>&& challenge_label,\nL144:           F seed,\nL145:           bool little_endian = true)\nL146:             : m_hasher(std::move(hasher)), m_domain_separator_label(domain_label), m_round_poly_label(poly_label),\nL147:               m_round_challenge_label(challenge_label), m_little_endian(little_endian), m_seed_rng(seed)\nL148:     `\nL149:   * Constructor with `const char*` arguments for labels:\nL150: \nL151:     `    SumcheckTranscriptConfig(\nL152:           Hash hasher,\nL153:           const char* domain_label,\nL154:           const char* poly_label,\nL155:           const char* challenge_label,\nL156:           F seed,\nL157:           bool little_endian = true)\nL158:             : m_hasher(std::move(hasher)), m_domain_separator_label(cstr_to_bytes(domain_label)),\nL159:               m_round_poly_label(cstr_to_bytes(poly_label)), m_round_challenge_label(cstr_to_bytes(challenge_label)),\nL160:               m_little_endian(little_endian), m_seed_rng(seed)\nL161:     `\nL162: ### Generating Sumcheck Proofscite62†​ L163: \nL164: To generate a proof, first, an empty proof needs to be created. The Sumcheck proof is represented by the `SumcheckProof<S>` class:\nL165: \nL166:     `template <typename S>\nL167:     class SumcheckProof\nL168:     `\nL169: \nL170: The class has a default constructor `SumcheckProof()` that takes no arguments.\nL171: The proof can be generated using the get_proof method from the `Sumcheck<F>` object:\nL172: \nL173:     `eIcicleError get_proof(\nL174:       const std::vector<F*>& mle_polynomials,\nL175:       const uint64_t mle_polynomial_size,\nL176:       const F& claimed_sum,\nL177:       const ReturningValueProgram<F>& combine_function,\nL178:       const SumcheckTranscriptConfig<F>&& transcript_config,\nL179:       const SumcheckConfig& sumcheck_config,\nL180:       SumcheckProof<F>& sumcheck_proof /*out*/) const\nL181:     `\nL182: \nL183: The arguments for this method are:\nL184:   * `mle_polynomials: std::vector<F*>&`: A vector of pointers, each pointing to the evaluations of one of the input polynomials.\nL185:   * `mle_polynomial_size: uint64_t`: The length of the polynomials (number of evaluations). This should be a power of 2.\nL186:   * `claimed_sum: F&`: The sum the Prover claims to be the sum of the combine function over the evaluations of the polynomials.\nL187:   * `combine_function: ReturningValueProgram<F>&`: The combine function, using ICICLE's cite20†program API.\nL188:   * `transcript_config: SumcheckTranscriptConfig<F>&&`: The configuration for the Fiat-Shamir scheme.\nL189:   * `sumcheck_config: SumcheckConfig&`: The configuration for the Sumcheck protocol.\nL190:   * `sumcheck_proof: SumcheckProof<F>&`: The output `SumcheckProof` object containing the generated proof.\nL191: #### Example: Generating a Proofcite63†​ L192: \nL193:     `auto prover_sumcheck = create_sumcheck<scalar_t>();\nL194:     SumcheckTranscriptConfig<scalar_t> transcript_config; // default configuration\nL195:     ReturningValueProgram<scalar_t> combine_func(EQ_X_AB_MINUS_C);\nL196:     SumcheckConfig sumcheck_config;\nL197:     SumcheckProof<scalar_t> sumcheck_proof;\nL198:     ICICLE_CHECK(prover_sumcheck.get_proof(\nL199:       mle_polynomials, mle_poly_size, claimed_sum, combine_func, std::move(transcript_config), sumcheck_config,\nL200:       sumcheck_proof));\nL201:     `\nL202: ### Verifying Sumcheck Proofscite64†​ L203: \nL204: To verify the proof, the Verifier should use the verify method of the `Sumcheck<F>` object:\nL205: \nL206:     `eIcicleError verify(\nL207:       const SumcheckProof<F>& sumcheck_proof,\nL208:       const F& claimed_sum,\nL209:       const SumcheckTranscriptConfig<F>&& transcript_config,\nL210:       bool& valid /*out*/)\nL211:     `\nL212: \nL213: The arguments for this method are:\nL214:   * `sumcheck_proof: SumcheckProof<F>&`: The proof that the Verifier wants to verify.\nL215:   * `claimed_sum: F&`: The sum that the Verifier wants to check, claimed by the Prover.\nL216:   * `transcript_config: SumcheckTranscriptConfig<F>&&`: The configuration for the Fiat-Shamir scheme.\nL217:   * `valid: bool`: The output of the method. `true` if the proof is valid, `false` otherwise.\nL218: \nL219: > NOTE: The `SumcheckTranscriptConfig` used for generating the proof must be identical to the one used for verification.\nL220: #### Example: Verifying a Proofcite65†​ L221: \nL222:     `auto verifier_sumcheck = create_sumcheck<scalar_t>();\nL223:     bool verification_pass = false;\nL224:     ICICLE_CHECK(\nL225:       verifier_sumcheck.verify(sumcheck_proof, claimed_sum, std::move(transcript_config), verification_pass));\nL226:     `\nL227: \nL228: After calling `verifier_sumcheck.verify`, the variable `verification_pass` will be `true` if the proof is valid, and `false` if not.\nL229: \nL230: cite66†Edit this page†github.com L231: \nL232: Last updated on Aug 6, 2025\nL233: \nL234: cite23†Previous Merkle-Tree cite24†Next FRI L235:   * cite55†Overview L236:     * cite56†Sumcheck with Combine Function L237:   * cite57†ICICLE's Sumecheck Implementation L238:     * cite58†Implementation Limitations L239:   * cite59†C++ API L240:     * cite60†SumcheckConfig L241:     * cite61†SumcheckTranscriptConfig L242:     * cite62†Generating Sumcheck Proofs "
